.TH GXWI-PACKAGE-MANAGER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-package-manager 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-package-manager \- install, update and remove software, and choose where it comes from, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-package-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Package Manager is the desktop's window on
.BR peipkg (1).
Open it from the launcher (type package).
It takes no arguments.
It has no authority of its own: it runs peipkg as the person, so what they can install or remove is exactly what they could with the command, and every change is the same transaction, with the same history and audit record.
.PP
Installed lists every installed package, with its version and where it came from; one opened shows its details, what it needs and provides, and its files, with Check Files, Update to and Remove.
Available lists what the repositories offer that isn't installed, with Install, and Install from File for a .peipkg file, which opens the file dialog.
Updates shows the newer versions waiting, after refreshing the trusted repositories, with Update and Update All.
History lists every change, newest first, with Undo for the most recent and Resolve for one that was interrupted.
Repositories lists each repository, with Refresh All, Add Repository, Trust and Remove.
Roles shows each claim and what holds it, and moves it to another package.
Installation Roots shows the named roots; they are added with peipkg root.
.PP
Every change goes the same way.
First the plan: what will be installed, updated, moved back or removed, before anything happens.
Then each elevated action on its own, such as a downgrade or a package taking over another's files, asked with Allow This; the approval is recorded in the audit stream.
Then the plan's own question, then progress, then the outcome, with anything peipkg warned of.
A removal can stop to ask about a configuration file changed since it was installed.
Once the plan is approved, the change finishes even if the window is closed.
.PP
Some refusals have a way on, offered once: Continue Anyway when a repository's information is out of date, Replace It when a file that belongs to no package is in the way, and Update Anyway when the package's publisher says it is updated another way.
Package Manager never confirms a repository's trust for the person: Trust shows the signing key and runs the trust ceremony only when they confirm it, and update checks refresh only repositories already trusted.
.PP
It asks the system what the person can do.
If they cannot read peipkg's records, every section says so once and shows nothing more; as shipped, only Administrators can.
If they can read them but not change them, everything is shown and nothing can be changed, with the reason said once.
.SH SECURITY
All package queries and transactions run through peipkg as the requester.
Repository trust and elevated transaction actions require explicit approvals.
Closing a window after approval does not cancel an ongoing transaction.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-package-manager.toml
Its application declaration.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/peipkg
What it runs for every query and change.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gxwi-file-dialog
The file dialog, which Install from File opens.
.TP
.I /var/state/peipkg/db.sqlite
.TQ
.I /var/state/peipkg/lock
peipkg's database and lock, which it opens, changing nothing, to learn whether the person can read and change what is installed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peipkg (1),
.BR gxwi-file-dialog (1).
.PP
Package Manager, at learn.peios.org.
